<p>Google wants to see a real person behind the site, not a bot churning out automated articles. Before applying, I made sure three basic but important pages were live and linked in both the header and the footer. An About page explained who I am and who the site is for, written in my own voice so it did not look like generic AI text. A Contact page gave visitors and brands a way to reach me, which signals there is a human running the show. A Privacy Policy explained how I use cookies, analytics, email forms, and advertising. You can generate a base policy with a free tool and then customize it to match your site. These three pages are what Google’s reviewers actively look for, so do not skip them.</p>
<h2>2. Added a Clear Navigation Menu</h2>
<p>For AdSense, your site should feel simple, clean, and easy to move around — both for real people and for Google’s reviewers. I used a main navigation bar with a dropdown that sends visitors straight to my main categories, and I repeated the same links in the footer of every page. This makes it trivial for someone to land on the site and instantly find the kind of content they care about instead of clicking around randomly and bouncing. A smooth, user-friendly experience is exactly what the program rewards, and it keeps visitors on the site longer, which also helps ad performance once you are approved.</p>
