        'meta_title' => 'Copywriting 101: Write Words That Sell',
        'meta_description' => 'Learn the fundamentals of copywriting — from hooks to calls to action — that make your words convert.',
        'content' => '<p>Copywriting is writing that is meant to persuade. Whether it is an email or a product page, the goal is a yes. Here are the fundamentals that make words convert, explained plainly so you can use them today.</p>
<h2>Know the Reader</h2>
<p>You cannot persuade strangers you do not understand. Name their problem, fears, and desires before you write a word. The more specific the reader in your head, the more personal the copy feels, and personal copy outperforms generic copy every time because it sounds like it was written for one person.</p>
<h2>Open With a Hook</h2>
<p>The first line decides if the rest gets read. Lead with a bold claim, a question, or a relatable pain. “Still paying full price for groceries?” beats “Welcome to our blog” because it interrupts the scroll with relevance. Hooks are not tricks; they are matching the reader’s attention to their need.</p>
<h2>Focus on Benefits</h2>
<p>Features tell, benefits sell. “Lightweight” is a feature; “carry it all day without back pain” is the benefit. Translate every feature into what it does for the reader, because people buy outcomes, not specifications. A spec sheet informs; a benefit persuades.</p>
<h2>Build Trust</h2>
<p>Proof — testimonials, numbers, guarantees — removes doubt. Doubt is the enemy of the sale, so answer it before the reader can even formulate the objection. A single specific result often does more than a paragraph of praise, because specifics feel real.</p>
<h2>Clear Call to Action</h2>
<p>Tell them exactly what to do: “Get started,” “Download,” “Buy now.” One action per piece. A confused reader does nothing, so remove every extra choice and make the next step obvious and low-friction. The CTA is where intent becomes action.</p>
<h2>Edit Ruthlessly</h2>
<p>Cut every word that does not earn its place. Tight writing feels confident and keeps attention. Read your draft aloud; if you stumble, the sentence is too long. Editing is where good copy becomes great, because clarity is what carries the persuasion across.</p>
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<p><strong>Is copywriting the same as writing?</strong> No — copywriting aims to persuade, not just to inform or entertain.</p>
<p><strong>Do I need a degree?</strong> No; practice and study of real ads teach more than school.</p>
<p><strong>What is the best exercise?</strong> Rewrite an ad you admire in your own words to learn its structure.</p>
<h2>Practice Daily</h2>
<p>Copywriting is a muscle; the more you write with intent, the faster the skill compounds across everything you publish, from emails to product pages to social posts.</p>'
