        'content' => '<p>A landing page has one job: get the visitor to take one action. Everything else is distraction. Here is the anatomy of one that converts, whether you are capturing leads or making sales.</p>
<h2>Headline That Promises</h2>
<p>Say the outcome clearly: “Get Your First Affiliate Sale in 30 Days.” Specific beats clever because the reader instantly knows if it is for them. The headline is the most important element; if it does not resonate, nothing below gets read, so spend real time on it.</p>
<h2>Subheadline for Clarity</h2>
<p>Add the who and how: “A step-by-step plan for complete beginners — no tech skills needed.” The subhead catches anyone the headline intrigued but left unsure, and it qualifies the right visitor while gently filtering the wrong one.</p>
<h2>Benefit Bullets</h2>
<p>Short, skimmable points that answer “what is in it for me?” Lead each with the outcome, not the feature. Readers skim; bullets respect that and let them self-select into yes before they reach the button. Each bullet should make saying no slightly harder.</p>
<h2>Social Proof</h2>
<p>A quote, a number, or a logo. Proof answers the silent question: “Can I trust this?” Even a single result from a real user lifts conversion noticeably, because trust is the gate that stands between interest and action.</p>
<h2>Single CTA</h2>
<p>One button, repeated if the page is long. Remove menu links so there is nowhere else to go. Every extra link is an exit ramp that bleeds conversions, so the page should feel like a guided path with one destination.</p>
<h2>Reduce Friction</h2>
<p>Short forms, clear pricing, and a reassuring note (“no card required”) lower resistance. The easier the yes, the more yeses you get. Friction is anything that makes the visitor think twice; your job is to remove it wherever possible.</p>
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<p><strong>How long should the page be?</strong> As long as needed to overcome doubt, but no longer; trim the rest.</p>
<p><strong>One CTA only?</strong> Yes for the primary action; secondary links dilute focus.</p>
<p><strong>What improves conversion most?</strong> Matching the headline to the ad or source that sent the visitor.</p>
<h2>Test One Thing at a Time</h2>
<p>Change the headline, then the button color, then the proof — separately. Testing everything at once tells you nothing. Let conversions guide the next edit, and the page keeps improving long after you first publish it.</p>'
